CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 15 ----------
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "What is shared between threads of one process, and what is private?", "Shared: the address space and everything in it, the descriptor table, credentials, the current directory, signal dispositions, resource limits, cgroup and namespaces. Private: registers including the instruction pointer, the stack, the thread ID, the signal mask, errno, priority, and thread-local storage."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Why can threads pass data in nanoseconds and also corrupt each other?", "Both come from the same fact — a shared address space with no boundary between them. You cannot take one property without the other."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "What flags turn <code>clone()</code> into a thread rather than a process?", "<code>CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND | CLONE_THREAD</code> — share the address space, filesystem context, descriptors, signal handlers and thread group. Linux schedules <em>tasks</em>; processes and threads are both tasks."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "What is fatal about the N:1 green-thread model?", "If one user thread makes a blocking system call, the kernel blocks the whole process and every other thread stops, because the kernel cannot know they exist. 1:1 (Linux NPTL, 2003) gives each user thread a real kernel task."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Where do Go's goroutines and Java's virtual threads sit?", "M:N on top of 1:1 — a runtime scheduler multiplexing many lightweight threads onto a pool of kernel threads, crucially intercepting blocking operations so a blocked goroutine parks rather than blocking its thread. They are an event loop hidden inside the runtime, with a different ergonomic choice."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "List the threads in a busy Node process.", "The main thread running the event loop and all your JavaScript; four libuv threadpool threads by default; V8's background threads for concurrent marking, sweeping and compilation; and any threads created by native addons."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "How does knowing which thread is busy localise a Node performance problem?", "Busy main thread = a blocked event loop, and the fix is in your code. Four busy threadpool threads with an idle main thread = threadpool saturation, and the fix is sizing or offloading. Busy V8 background threads = GC pressure, and the fix is allocation behaviour."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Why does a <code>worker_thread</code> get its own V8 isolate rather than sharing objects?", "JavaScript has no memory model for concurrently mutated objects, and a garbage collector that moves objects cannot safely do so while another thread holds raw pointers. The restriction is forced by the language, not chosen for simplicity."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Distinguish <code>postMessage</code>, <code>transferList</code> and <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code>.", "<code>postMessage</code> deep-copies via structured clone — expensive for large payloads and unable to carry functions or prototypes. <code>transferList</code> moves an <code>ArrayBuffer</code>: ownership passes, the sender's becomes detached, constant time regardless of size. <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> is genuinely shared pages — the one place JavaScript has real shared mutable memory, and the one place it can have a data race."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Give the rule for choosing between the four concurrency options in Node.", "Workers for CPU-bound work when you want cores (a pool created at boot, never one per request — each costs a V8 isolate). The threadpool for blocking I/O. A separate process for isolation or a separate heap. The event loop for everything else."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Why do Web Workers have no DOM?", "The DOM is a large mutable object graph with no locking and no memory model; permitting concurrent access would require synchronisation on every DOM operation. So the platform's position is that the DOM is single-threaded, permanently, and <code>OffscreenCanvas</code> and <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> exist to make the boundary less painful."),
("node-15", "Node 15 · Threads", "Postgres chose processes and MySQL chose threads. What does each buy?", "Postgres gets crash isolation and must arrange shared memory explicitly (<code>shared_buffers</code> is a shared mapping). MySQL gets cheaper connection creation and a naturally shared buffer pool, at the cost that one backend corrupting memory takes the server down. Neither is wrong, and the choice predicts connection-scaling behaviour."),
# ---------- Node 16 ----------
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Why is <code>counter++</code> not atomic?", "It compiles to three instructions — load, add, store — and two threads can interleave between any two of them. Both load 5, both compute 6, both store 6: two increments, one net effect. Nothing was written incorrectly."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Define atomic, race condition, and data race.", "Atomic: an operation no other thread can observe partway through. Race condition: the result depends on the relative timing of operations that were not made atomic. Data race: two threads accessing the same location with at least one write and no synchronisation — in C/C++ formally undefined behaviour, so the compiler may assume it never happens."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Why must concurrency correctness be established by reasoning rather than testing?", "Races are timing-dependent and do not reproduce. One in ten million iterations passes every test on a laptop and fails twice a week in production, sensitive to core count, load, compiler version, and whether a profiler is attached."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "What is cache coherence and what does it cost?", "Each core has its own L1/L2, so hardware must keep copies consistent — MESI, where a line is Modified, Exclusive, Shared or Invalid. To write, a core must broadcast an invalidation and wait for every other core to drop its copy: tens to hundreds of cycles over the interconnect."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "What is false sharing and why is it invisible in a profile?", "Two threads writing to two <em>different</em> variables in the same 64-byte cache line bounce that line between cores on every write, because coherence works at line granularity. The code is correct and throughput collapses by an order of magnitude. The fix is padding, which is why high-performance structures are littered with alignment attributes."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Why can identical concurrent code be correct on x86 and broken on ARM?", "Memory ordering. x86's TSO model only lets a store appear after a subsequent load; ARM and POWER have weak models where almost anything can be reordered. This stopped being niche when Apple Silicon and Graviton became ordinary development and deployment targets."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "How does the compiler break concurrent code that looks correct?", "It hoists loop-invariant reads, keeps variables in registers instead of re-reading memory, and merges or eliminates stores — all valid under the assumption that no other thread is looking. A loop polling a flag can be optimised into an infinite loop."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "What is compare-and-swap and why is it called universal?", "Atomically: if this location holds the value I expect, replace it and tell me whether it did. <code>lock cmpxchg</code> on x86, load-linked/store-conditional on ARM. Every lock, lock-free queue, reference counter and atomic increment is built from it."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Recognise optimistic concurrency at three scales.", "The CAS retry loop in hardware; a Postgres <code>UPDATE ... WHERE version = $expected</code> retried on zero rows affected; and an HTTP <code>If-Match</code> with an ETag. Same pattern, same failure mode — under high contention the retries dominate and you need a lock."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "What exactly does JavaScript's single thread protect you from?", "Data races only. Run-to-completion means nothing observes or modifies your objects while your synchronous code runs, so <code>counter++</code> is safe. It does not protect you from race conditions."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Why is a read-check-write across an <code>await</code> a race?", "Every <code>await</code> is a yield point: the function suspends, the loop runs other callbacks including another invocation of the same function, and it resumes against state that may have changed. Two concurrent transfers can both read balance 100, both pass the check, and both write 50."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Give the three correct fixes for that transfer bug.", "Make it atomic in the database (<code>UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - $1 WHERE id = $2 AND balance &gt;= $1</code>, checking the row count); take a lock (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> in a transaction, or an advisory lock); or use a version column with a retry. The same fixes a threaded program would use."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "What is a cache stampede and why is storing the promise the fix?", "<code>if (!cache.has(k)) cache.set(k, await fetch(k))</code> issues N concurrent fetches for N concurrent requests, because every check happens before any set. Storing the promise means the second caller finds one in flight and awaits it. Recognising it as check-then-act is what tells you the fix."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "Why does <code>Atomics</code> exist in JavaScript, and why is <code>Atomics.wait</code> banned on the browser main thread?", "It exists because <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> is genuinely shared memory and therefore the one place JavaScript can have a data race; the operations compile to the <code>lock</code>-prefixed instructions. <code>Atomics.wait</code> blocks, which would freeze the page."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "In what sense is a database isolation level a memory model?", "Both are specifications of which interleavings a participant may observe. Read committed permits non-repeatable reads, the database equivalent of a compiler not being required to re-read a variable; Postgres <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> uses optimistic concurrency and can abort you with a serialization failure requiring the same retry loop as CAS."),
("node-16", "Node 16 · Races", "How is an idempotency key a compare-and-swap?", "A retry racing an original is the same interleaving problem; checking and inserting the key atomically is compare-and-swap over the network. Once you have seen the pattern at the instruction level the distributed version needs no separate learning."),
# ---------- Node 17 ----------
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "When is a spin lock right and when is it catastrophic?", "Right when hold time is shorter than a context switch — a few hundred nanoseconds — and there are more cores than contenders, which is true inside a kernel and almost never in application code. Catastrophic on a single core: the spinner cannot progress until the holder runs, and the holder cannot run until the spinner is preempted."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why is <em>test and test-and-set</em> better than plain test-and-set?", "Spinning on a write means each attempt acquires the cache line exclusively, saturating the interconnect and slowing the actual holder. Spinning on a plain read can be satisfied from a shared line, and only the final attempt writes."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "What is the futex's key property?", "The uncontended path never enters the kernel. Acquire is a compare-and-swap in user space — a few nanoseconds; only on failure does it call <code>futex(FUTEX_WAIT, ...)</code> to sleep on a kernel wait queue keyed by that address. Release wakes only if it can see a waiter."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why is a high <code>futex</code> count in <code>strace -c</code> meaningful?", "Because uncontended locks make no system calls at all, so futex calls are direct evidence of genuine contention."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "What is a semaphore and how does it relate to a mutex?", "Dijkstra, 1965: a counter with atomic decrement-and-wait-if-negative and increment-and-wake. Initialised to N it permits N holders, which makes it the natural expression of a resource pool. A mutex is the case N=1."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why must a condition variable always be waited on in a <code>while</code> loop?", "Because of spurious wakeups, and because another thread may have consumed the condition before you were scheduled."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Name Coffman's four conditions for deadlock and say which one you break.", "Mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and circular wait. In practice you break circular wait, with <strong>lock ordering</strong>: define a total order over all locks and always acquire in it — which is why multi-row database code should sort identifiers first."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Distinguish deadlock, livelock, starvation and the thundering herd.", "Deadlock: nothing runs. Livelock: threads run and respond to each other but nothing completes. Starvation: a thread never gets the resource because others keep taking it. Thundering herd: releasing wakes every waiter, all of whom race and all but one sleep again — hence waking one."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why is Redis single-threaded, and what is the price?", "Every command is atomic by construction — no locks, races, deadlock or coherence traffic — which is why <code>INCR</code> is a correct distributed counter and a Lua script is atomic. The price is that one slow command blocks everything (<code>KEYS *</code> on a large database is an outage, hence <code>SCAN</code>) and that commands use one core. Redis 6 threaded the socket I/O while keeping execution single-threaded."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "How do you diagnose a lock wait in Postgres?", "<code>pg_stat_activity</code>: <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code> means waiting on another transaction, <code>'LWLock'</code> means internal shared memory. <code>pg_blocking_pids()</code> names the blocker. Postgres detects deadlocks after <code>deadlock_timeout</code> (1 s) and aborts one transaction with an error you can catch and retry — unusually generous; most systems just hang."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why is a distributed lock not a lock?", "Nothing guarantees the holder has not vanished. A GC pause, CPU throttling or a partition can exceed the lease, after which two processes believe they hold it. The fix is a <strong>fencing token</strong> — a monotonically increasing number passed with every write and rejected by the storage layer if lower than the highest seen. Without fencing it reduces contention rather than guaranteeing correctness."),
("node-17", "Node 17 · Locks", "Why does <code>async-mutex</code> work without atomics, and where does it fail?", "It is a promise chain, and JavaScript's run-to-completion means the acquire check and set cannot be interleaved. It is correct within one Node process and gives you exactly nothing across cluster workers or replicas."),
# ---------- Node 18 ----------
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "What actually happens when a process blocks on <code>read</code>?", "The syscall enters the kernel; the socket buffer is empty; the task's state becomes S; it is added to that socket's wait queue; the scheduler picks something else. Later a packet arrives, the kernel walks the wait queue and marks the task runnable, and it eventually copies the bytes and returns."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Correct the claim that blocking wastes CPU.", "A blocked process consumes exactly zero processor time. The cost is not cycles — it is that this thread can do nothing else, so many clients need many threads, and threads are the expensive thing."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Name the three pre-event-loop server architectures and what each traded.", "Process per connection (inetd, Apache prefork): robust, a crash contained, and expensive. Thread per connection (Apache worker, classic servlet containers): cheaper, still a stack and a scheduler entry each. Bounded thread pool: bounds resource use and introduces head-of-line blocking."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Run the C10K arithmetic for thread-per-connection.", "~100 KB per thread all-in is 1 GB of memory before any application data; ten thousand tasks in the scheduler; and at ten events per second per connection, 100,000 wake-ups per second at a few microseconds each — a large fraction of a core spent purely switching. Worst of all, the caches never hold anything useful."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "State the event loop's advantage and its exact boundary.", "One thread learns about many ready connections in one call and handles each with a function call: nanoseconds and warm caches, and a few hundred bytes per connection instead of ~100 KB. It says nothing about computation — if each event needs 10 ms of processing, one thread handles a hundred per second."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Why was nginx written, and what did it beat Apache at?", "Igor Sysoev, released October 2004, specifically against C10K: an event-driven architecture with one worker process per core. Under many idle or slow connections — which is what the real internet is — it used a fraction of the memory and stayed responsive where Apache thrashed."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "What was Ryan Dahl's stated argument for Node.js in 2009?", "That Apache's concurrency model was wrong, and that existing languages made non-blocking code painful because they had blocking standard libraries and no natural callback idiom. JavaScript had no I/O library to deprecate and already had closures."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "What makes Node's non-blocking guarantee actually hold?", "That there is no alternative — a library cannot accidentally block the loop with a blocking read because the blocking read is not exposed. Except the <code>Sync</code> functions, which is why using them outside startup is the cardinal error."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Why is a database connection pool a bounded thread pool?", "It has all the same properties: 20 connections serving 200 concurrent requests means 180 waiting, invisibly, inside your process; and one slow query holds a connection while everything behind it waits — head-of-line blocking. Instrument acquisition wait time and queue depth, use statement timeouts, and separate pools for fast and slow work."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Why does Slowloris defeat thread-per-connection and not an event loop?", "Each slowly-held connection occupies a thread in the first design, so a few hundred exhaust the pool with almost no bandwidth. In an event loop each costs a socket and a few hundred bytes. Header and body timeouts still exist because the resource is small but not zero."),
("node-18", "Node 18 · Blocking I/O", "Why is a 200 ms <code>JSON.parse</code> categorically different in Node than in a threaded server?", "In thread-per-connection it delays one client. In an event loop it delays every client, because there is one thread and it is not preemptible. Measuring it is what <code>monitorEventLoopDelay</code> is for — the single most valuable metric a Node service can export."),
# ---------- Node 19 ----------
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Why is a loop over ten thousand non-blocking descriptors not a solution?", "It makes ten thousand system calls per pass and almost all return <code>EAGAIN</code>. At 50–100 ns each, one pass costs a millisecond of pure overhead and typically finds nothing."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Name <code>select</code>'s three structural problems.", "<code>FD_SETSIZE</code> is 1024, compiled into the bitmap type; you rebuild and re-pass the entire set every call because the call destroys it, and the kernel copies and scans all of it; and the return tells you how many are ready, not which, so you scan all n again."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "State the wrong scaling property in one sentence.", "The cost is proportional to the number of connections you have rather than the number of events that happened — which is exactly wrong for servers holding many mostly-idle connections."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "What did <code>poll</code> fix and what did it leave?", "It replaced the fixed-size bitmap with an array that is not destroyed, so there is no 1024 limit and no rebuilding. The array is still passed in full, copied, and scanned every call. An ergonomic improvement, not an architectural one."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "How does epoll achieve O(1), mechanically?", "Registering a descriptor attaches a callback to that socket's wait queue. When the network softirq appends data, that callback pushes the descriptor onto the epoll instance's ready list. So the list is maintained continuously by the events themselves, and <code>epoll_wait</code> does not scan — it hands back the list."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Distinguish level-triggered from edge-triggered, and say who uses which.", "Level-triggered reports readiness as long as data remains, so a partial read is safe. Edge-triggered reports only the transition, so failing to drain to <code>EAGAIN</code> means the connection hangs silently — faster, and unforgiving. libuv is level-triggered; nginx is edge-triggered."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Name the four platform mechanisms libuv hides.", "epoll (Linux), kqueue (BSD/macOS, Jonathan Lemon, FreeBSD 4.1, 2000 — arguably a better design, handling timers, signals and file events through one interface), IOCP (Windows, completion-based rather than readiness-based), and Solaris event ports."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Why can a regular file not be polled for readiness?", "Readiness answers 'will this block?', and for a file the answer is always that you can read it — the real question is how long, which readiness cannot express. epoll returns <code>EPERM</code> for regular files, and <code>O_NONBLOCK</code> is simply ignored on them."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Given that, what are the only three possible responses, and which did Node take?", "Use threads (libuv's threadpool — Node's answer, and the reason <code>UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE</code> exists); use POSIX <code>aio</code>, widely regarded as broken; or use <code>io_uring</code>, a completion-based interface that works properly, which libuv adopted for some file operations from 1.45 (2023)."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "What does <code>strace</code> on an idle Node server show, and what does that tell you?", "One line: a blocked <code>epoll_wait</code>. That is the entire runtime at rest, consuming zero CPU. Under load it becomes <code>epoll_wait</code> returning, a burst of <code>read</code>/<code>write</code>/<code>futex</code>, and back."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "Why can a slow DNS server stall your file reads?", "<code>dns.lookup</code> calls <code>getaddrinfo</code>, a blocking library function, so it runs on the shared libuv threadpool alongside file I/O, crypto and zlib. Four threads by default. Use <code>dns.resolve</code>, which speaks DNS over a socket and goes through epoll."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "What is <code>EMFILE</code>, why is 1024 the classic limit, and how do you diagnose it?", "The per-process descriptor table is full. 1024 is a fossil of <code>select</code>'s <code>FD_SETSIZE</code>. Diagnose with <code>ls /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd | wc -l</code> and <code>lsof -p</code>; usually it is a leak — unconsumed HTTP responses, unclosed streams, connections without keep-alive timeouts — and a leak exhausts any limit."),
("node-19", "Node 19 · epoll", "What is backpressure at the socket level, and what happens if you ignore it?", "A full send buffer makes a non-blocking <code>write</code> return short, and the correct response is to register for <code>EPOLLOUT</code>. Node surfaces this as <code>write()</code> returning <code>false</code> and a later <code>'drain'</code>. Ignoring it means Node buffers the unwritten data in your heap without limit — which is how streaming a large file to a slow client exhausts memory. <code>pipeline()</code> handles it, which is the real reason to prefer it."),
# ---------- Node 20 ----------
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "What kind of thing is the event loop, precisely?", "A scheduler in user space. Everything from Node 7 applies, with one decisive difference: it has no timer interrupt and no privileged mode, so it is cooperative and regains control only when a callback returns."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Name libuv's phases in order.", "timers; pending callbacks (deferred system operations, chiefly certain TCP errors); idle and prepare (internal); poll (the only phase that blocks); check (<code>setImmediate</code>); close callbacks."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "How is the poll timeout computed, in three cases?", "Zero if <code>setImmediate</code> callbacks are pending, since the check phase has work. The time until the nearest timer if any — which is how <code>setTimeout</code> is implemented, with no timer syscall and no timer thread. Otherwise infinite, which is why an idle Node process uses zero CPU."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Why does a Node process exit, and why does a forgotten <code>setInterval</code> prevent it?", "There is no 'main is finished' concept — the loop exits when nothing at all is pending: no handles, no requests, no timers. A live timer or an unclosed server is pending work."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Where do <code>process.nextTick</code> and microtasks run?", "Not in any phase — after every single callback and between every phase transition, drained completely including anything added while draining. <code>nextTick</code> first, entirely, then microtasks, entirely."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "How can <code>process.nextTick</code> hang a process?", "A <code>nextTick</code> that schedules another loops forever without the loop advancing: no I/O served, no timers, apparently hung at 100% CPU. The same is true of a recursively resolving promise chain. <code>setImmediate</code> cannot do this because it lands in the next iteration's check phase."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Where does the code after an <code>await</code> run relative to timers and I/O?", "Before both. The continuation is queued as a microtask, so <code>await</code> is a very cheap yield rather than a trip through the loop. <code>await</code> on a non-promise still yields and still costs a microtask turn."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Why is <code>setTimeout(f,0)</code> vs <code>setImmediate(g)</code> nondeterministic at the top level but deterministic inside an I/O callback?", "At the top level the 1 ms clamp may or may not have elapsed by the time the loop first reaches timers, depending on startup duration. Inside an I/O callback the loop is in the poll phase and check comes immediately after, while timers are a full iteration away — so <code>setImmediate</code> always wins."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Describe one iteration of the browser's event loop.", "Take <em>one</em> task from a task queue and run it to completion; perform a microtask checkpoint, draining that queue entirely; then, if it is time to render, run the rendering steps."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Name the browser's rendering steps in order.", "<code>requestAnimationFrame</code> callbacks; recalculate style; layout; update the compositing tree; paint. Then <code>requestIdleCallback</code> if time remains before the next frame."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Why is <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> the correct place for visual changes and <code>setTimeout(f,16)</code> not?", "rAF runs immediately before style and layout, so changes are applied in the same frame with no intermediate paint. A timer lands in a task queue with no relationship to the frame boundary, producing jitter and dropped frames."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "What is layout thrashing and why does batching fix it?", "Reading a geometric property (<code>offsetHeight</code>, <code>getBoundingClientRect</code>, <code>scrollTop</code>) forces the browser to flush pending style and layout to answer correctly. Alternating writes and reads forces a layout per iteration; all reads then all writes makes it one."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Why does React's scheduler use <code>MessageChannel</code> rather than <code>setTimeout</code>?", "To avoid the 4 ms clamp on nested timeouts. It yields roughly every 5 ms so the browser can take input and paint — a cooperative scheduler inside a cooperative scheduler inside a preemptive one."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "What are event loop delay and event loop utilisation, and why export both?", "Delay is the time between when a callback was due and when it ran — it tells you whether the loop is being blocked and by how much. Utilisation is the fraction of time the loop spent working rather than waiting in <code>epoll_wait</code> — it tells you how close to saturated the process is, and is a better autoscaling signal than CPU. A p99 delay of 200 ms at 40% CPU is a complete diagnosis."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "List everything that blocks the event loop.", "Any <code>*Sync</code> call; <code>JSON.parse</code>/<code>stringify</code> on a large object; synchronous crypto or compression; a regular expression with catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS); a large synchronous loop; a deep <code>structuredClone</code>; template rendering of a large page; and a very large number of pending microtasks, since they drain fully before the loop advances."),
("node-20", "Node 20 · The event loop", "Match the fixes to the causes.", "Chunk with <code>setImmediate</code> to yield between chunks; move CPU-bound work to a worker thread; move heavy or slow work to a separate process or a queue; stream instead of buffering; or push it into the database if it is really a data operation. Raising <code>UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE</code> does nothing for main-thread blocking."),
]
